#import "../../typst-ko/lib.typ": *

This part does not yet teach how to compute anything in Lowent. Instead it lays down *the place where you first meet this language*: why such a
language is needed (#chref("intro")), how to build and run the tool and get rejected by it (#chref("first-program")), and the rules of the surface
that all code follows (#chref("surface")).

*The goal* (#chref("intro")) --- sets the goal of knowing what an op can and cannot do from its head alone, and the five ideas that hold it up.

*The tool* (#chref("first-program")) --- runs the same program on the VM and natively and compares them, and shows contracts stopping execution and how
to read diagnostic codes.

*The surface* (#chref("surface")) --- the detached period, `do … end` blocks, prefix notation, and the clause order table for op heads. That table is used
to the end of the book.
